\section{Covariates that vary over time}{

A forecast needs the future values of every covariate the model uses. For
some they are known in advance, such as a calendar, a planned dose or a
scheduled price. For others they are not, and there are three honest
choices:
\itemize{
\item plug in a scenario, and say that is what the forecast assumes;
\item forecast the covariate itself first;
\item supply several plausible paths, so that the forecast's uncertainty
includes the covariate's. \code{\link[=ilm_covariate_paths]{ilm_covariate_paths()}} draws them from an
illume model of the covariate, and \code{\link[=ilm_as_draws]{ilm_as_draws()}} takes them from
another tool.
}

\code{ilm_future()} makes none of these choices for you. It leaves those columns
missing and names them in a message. A forecast made with realised future
values of a covariate is \emph{ex-post}: in fpp3's terms (section 7.6), made
with later information on the predictors, not a genuine forecast. It
flatters the model, which is limn's own reading; see \code{\link[=ilm_folds]{ilm_folds()}}.
}

\section{Time steps}{

The step between time points is taken from the data unless you give
\code{step}.
\itemize{
\item For numeric time, it is the one positive difference the series share.
\item For dates, it is a calendar unit when every step is one: a day, a week,
a month, a quarter or a year. So monthly dates advance to the same day of
the next month, not by a fixed 30 days.
}

Irregular spacing has no single next time, so then \code{step} must be given.
}

